A||N||3||wriske||brake Problem||||1||
Z||000000||wriske||06-28-2000||03:03 PM||||I have installed new rear brake pads and master cylinder.  I have bled brakes numberous times.  No Air Leaks.  Still have spongy pads.  Need HELP FAST!!!||||reg||1
Z||000001||batboy||06-28-2000||04:15 PM||||It's real hard to get all the air out of the brake system when you've replaced the master cylinder unless you bench bleed the M/C first. Otherwise, keep bleeding.||||reg||1
Z||000002||BBBAD GT||06-28-2000||04:18 PM||||Keeeeeep bleeeeeeding.  I had to when I did the GA/Blazer brake/MC swap.  I think I went through 5 big bottles of fluid.  Get this, all of the pumping of the brakes drained my battery because of the third brake light on all of the time.  Now thats alot of bleeding.<P> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||06-28-2000||04:29 PM||||M.C.'s must be blead according to the included instructions before/when installing them. If you don't do this then it's likely to leave a spongy pedal.<P>You also need to thuroughly test the brake booster. It that or the lines or check valve are messed up then you'll also get spongy pedal.<P>A final option is to power blead or vac blead the system. These methods can surge fluid faster and knock loose any stuck air bubbles.||||reg||1
